hello i'm greg sadler and i would love  to tell you about a great class coming  up that i'm very excited about hosted on  the light hall platform called aristotle  on the moral virtues and this is  something i'll tell you about in just a  minute i've been studying for decades  and applying in my own life and with  clients i think it's very  powerful and valuable  materials ideas practices distinctions  that you can apply and i do want to  address something right off the bat i've  had people already asking me questions  since i posted the class is this just  for people who already have a solid  background in philosophy or could  beginners benefit from this and the  answer is yes you can come into this  class as a completely raw beginner just  somebody who has an interest in the  notion of good moral character and how  to improve our lives so as to have that  and benefit ourselves and those around  us through that you'll be reading  aristotle i'm going to be leading you  through the text i'm providing you with  lots of resources just as i would for my  academic students in  colleges and universities where i've  been teaching this material for you know  over two decades at this point so you  could be a complete beginner so long as  you have some interest and you know a  desire to  read your way through aristotle with a  guide you'll benefit from it if you've  had some background you will get  something out of this as well even if  you've been studying aristotle for some  time  you're going to find in this class that  there's going to be some pointers some  practices some ideas that you might not  have  noticed up until this point so we're  looking at one of the most important  texts in ethics and particularly in  virtue ethics  really for several millennia one of the  big thinkers aristotle and what are we  going to do in the class so each class  session we're going to meet for 90  minutes students are going to have the  opportunity at the very beginning of the  class to raise a few questions address  some confusion some of it might be  terminological some of it might be  conceptual and then we'll jump right  into looking at the material for that  week and we're going to be going through  nick mccann ethics books  2  into book 6 over this 8 week period so a  good portion of the text itself so i'll  talk about what aristotle is saying and  then we'll you know have some discussion  and some q a and hopefully everybody  comes away from every session  with a solid understanding of not just  what aristotle is saying  but how you would apply it in your own  life to change the ways that you  approach situations how you think about  things the responses that you have both  action and volitional but also emotional  so a lot of stuff packed into every  single one of these sessions i do want  to lead you through the  we call it the itinerary right so in  session one which will be on tuesday  june 28th at noon central time  general features of virtues and vices  we're going to be doing some sort of an  overview this will be the introductory  lesson and we'll focus on nick mccann  ethics book 2 where aristotle tells us  quite a bit about this then from  session two  through session six we're going to be  looking at specific  virtues or clusters of virtues in  each of the  portions of the nickel mccain ethic so  session two virtues of courage and  temperance that's nick of mccain ethics  book three session three is going to be  devoted specifically to one virtue the  virtue of good temper or gentleness  something i do a lot of research on  having to do with anger we'll spend an  entire session on that  session four we're going to be looking  at wealth and other resources and how we  allocate them  you know who we devote them to so we're  looking at generosity and magnificence  also coming from nico mccann ethics book  four session five we're following that  up virtues of right ambition and  magnanimity or great soldness things  that have to do with honor and ambition  and social status  then session six we're going to be doing  a triple header looking at the so-called  social virtues the virtues of  friendliness good humor and truthfulness  in session seven we're going to be  looking at a more complicated virtue and  a set of issues namely those concerned  with  justice and that will be looking at nick  mccain ethics book  five and then we'll wrap up by looking  at some of the nico mccann ethics book  six discussions of prudence or practical  wisdom  how it figures into the moral virtues  and helps to guide them what its  connection is with other  you know what aristotle calls  intellectual virtues and why we in fact  need prudence and that will be our final  session for this this class so i'm  really excited about this as i think  many of you know from having seen my  videos or you know hearing me on  podcasts or reading blog entries or  other things that i've been in  one of the things that i do is take  complex philosophical topics texts  thinkers  and i help to make them understandable i  make them accessible for lifelong  learners working professionals students  even other professors who sometimes  struggle with the stuff so what you can  be guaranteed is if you take this class  with me you are going to come out with a  really solid both intellectual and  practical  understanding of how the aristotelian  virtues work what they include and  extend to and how to avoid the vices  opposed to them on both sides of  deficiency  and access so this is going to be a  great class i hope you can join me for  it and i look forward to interacting  with all of you learners in just a few  weeks  you
 

